From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryson <10244083+brysonwf@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 15:23:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8150-microsoft-surface-duo: leave the
 Bluetooth address to userspace

A non-zero local-bd-address is applied again on every controller setup, so
it overwrote the phone's own address that userspace sets from the persist
partition (btmgmt public-addr triggers a re-setup). Use the all-zero value
like other Qualcomm phones: the controller stays unconfigured until
userspace provides the address, which the kernel then keeps.

This patch was written by Claude (Anthropic's AI assistant) under the
direction of Bryson, who tested it on his Surface Duo.

Signed-off-by: Bryson <10244083+brysonwf@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../dts/qcom/sm8150-microsoft-surface-duo-workbench.dtsi     | 5 ++---
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-microsoft-surface-duo-workbench.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-microsoft-surface-duo-workbench.dtsi
index f404c0798..ce3089795 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-microsoft-surface-duo-workbench.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-microsoft-surface-duo-workbench.dtsi
@@ -229,9 +229,8 @@ &uart13 {
 
 	bluetooth {
 		compatible = "qcom,wcn3998-bt";
-		/* fixed locally-administered address 02:D0:05:00:00:01 (little-endian); the Duo bootloader fills in none, and
-		 * without one the controller stays "unconfigured" (2026-11-03) */
-		local-bd-address = [ 01 00 00 05 d0 02 ];
+		/* the phone's own address is set by userspace from the persist partition (the bootloader fills in none) */
+		local-bd-address = [ 00 00 00 00 00 00 ];
 
 		vddio-supply = <&vreg_pmu_io>;
 		vddxo-supply = <&vreg_pmu_xo>;
